A||||11||Pike||Brake Pros:  HELP!||||1||
Z||000000||Pike||10-28-2001||09:31 PM||||87 GT:<P>chronology of event:<BR>1. front brakes dragging, will not fully disengage...no rear brake function.<BR>2. Brake pedle hard as rock.<BR>3. front brakes too hot to drive.<P>Troubleshoot:<BR>1. Resoivour full.<BR>2. Bleed rears...normal. brakes pump up.<BR>3. Front right brake found fully engaged. Open bleed valve and brake disengages as fluid squirts out of valve.<BR>4. Calliper will not pump up. Tried for an hour. No fluid entering calliper.<BR>5. Left front: Brake fully engaged. Open bleed valve and brake disengages. No fluid entering calliper.<BR>6. Checked master cylinder: Operation normal.<BR>7. Checked Hyd. distribution block. Pulled block and checked with compressed air for debri. No debri found.<P>I'm really stuck here. This is my daily driver so I'm hurt'in without the car. <P>Thanks,<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||10-28-2001||10:20 PM||||Sounds like the flexible hoses on the front calipers may be the culprit.  They have been known to swell or seperate internally and become blocked.  When this happens they can trap pressure at the caliper or prevent pressure from getting there.  New hoses would probably be a good investment.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pike||10-28-2001||10:48 PM||||Thanks Rod...my next step is to use compressed air and make sure I've got clear passage all the way to the calliper. Are brake parts like the hoses readily available?<BR>Thanks for the feedback!<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-29-2001||12:04 AM||||Don't use compressed air in brake systems!<P>It likely contains oil and/or water. Neither is good for the system. Oil will ruin even more parts.<P>Both flex lines is the most likely thing.<P>When you are done. flush the system with Valvoline Synthetic Brake Fluid or some other fluid with high WET boiling point. This will help push out any water you blew in there with the air hose. It won't undo problems from oil.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hiwil88formula||10-29-2001||12:11 AM||||I had this same problem on the front calipers of mine.  The problem was I tightened the brake hose on the caliper too tight.  So, I loosened it a bit and now they work fine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pike||10-29-2001||12:26 AM||||Why would tight hose bolts at the calliper cause the problem? Mine are fairly tight...not enough to gall anything...but they are tight. <BR>Is there anything about the differential braking that could be causing the problem? Is there anything that determines how much hydraulic pressure is applied to the front callipers?<P>Thanks for the warning about the compressed air...but at this point I have no choice but to use the tools I have available to troubleshoot this problem in the shortest amount of time possible.<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||10-29-2001||01:16 AM||||Get a set of SS braided lines from the Fierostore.<P>They're *CHEAPER* than OE replacements (yeah, $99 for the SS lines, like $160+ for OE...)  <P>Good stuff, and they won't collapse and lock your wheels anymore.<P>PS: if it's a bloody 88, or even not, replace the damn slider pins.<P>God I hate single-piston calipers!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||Jhonny||10-29-2001||01:59 AM||||I have encountered swollen brake hoses before<BR>not on a Fiero though. The symptoms is engegaged brakes and stiff brake pedal because of swollen front hoses. <BR>On old cars (Fiero ?!) the rubber starts to detoriate inside the hoses and can cause all kinds of trouble.<P>/// Jhonny<P>------------------<BR>Jhonny Sokura<BR>Vadstena, Östergötland Sweden<P><A HREF="http://home.swipnet.se/jks/fiero3.jpg" TARGET=_blank> Pontiac Fiero GT -87 , V6 5spd<BR> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||hiwil88formula||10-29-2001||03:05 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Pike:<BR>[B]Why would tight hose bolts at the calliper cause the problem? Mine are fairly tight...not enough to gall anything...but they are tight. <P>The bolt has a hole in it if it is turned too far the fluid does not have an opening to go through.  Just my thought, could be wrong.  Just make sure they are (snug) that is what I had to do to mine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||85gted||10-29-2001||03:34 AM||||If you make want to be shur about the brake hoses and that is what it sounds like,loosen the metal line at the front body mount to the brake hose and if it don't relive the calipers but if you loosen it at the caliper then it must be the brake hoses!!!!! ???||||reg||11
Z||000010||theogre||10-29-2001||12:40 PM||||He's already had the lines off the balance block and/or MC. If that didn't let them off then it's got to be the soft lines.<P>Also, If it was just the MC, when one bleeder was opened, both calipers would have released.<P>As long as that air line isn't used with tool oilers then the thing hopefully isn't blowing oil. That depends partly on compressor type and condition.<P>Almost all compressors blow some water, so still use fluid with high wet boil and flush the whole sytem with it. That should get rid of the bulk of water that may have gotten in there. Plus the fluid won't be as degraded like DOT 3 fluids listing the 284 wet boil.<P>There are 2 things that will kill the soft lines. <P>Oil in the sysytem... This of coarse ruins almost all the seals and hoses.<P>Oil on the jacket. The oil will soak thru the cracks common in old jackets and swell the lining from the outside. The lines still have to be replaced but at least the rest of the system is undamaged by this. These line should NEVER be washed or treated with anything containing petrolium distillates.<P>Banjo Bolts should not care what position the holes line up at. They are intentionally made to not care. If bolt "torque" made a differance then the problem would likely be the wrong bolts. Bolts could be too long or have the cross hole drilled too far from the head. Either could block the holes.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pike||10-30-2001||01:49 AM||||Hey ...thanks Ogre. I really appreciate the help. What I can't figure out...is how BOTH hoses failed symaltaneously???? ...and not the rear lines?? Something is wrong with this picture...it's like I'm too close to the problem to see the solution. OK...tomorrow night I'll flow check both front lines from the hoses to the distribution block. I'll let you know tomorrow night what I find. So long for tonight.<BR>Pike||||reg||1
